// Real explanations for the NVTI core questions (replaces the short "Answer: ..." notes). Matched by question text.
(function(){const E={"First action for a person in contact with live equipment…":"Break the contact first, using the supply switch or a dry insulator, so you do not become a second casualty. Never grab the person with bare hands.","Before touching a conductor you must…":"A voltage indicator must be a two-pole tester, proved on a known live source before and after. A single test cannot be trusted if the tester itself is faulty.","A straight ladder is set at a safety ratio of about…":"A ladder at about 75° (1 out for every 4 up) is stable and resists slipping. Too shallow slides out, too steep topples back.","The standard BS symbol for an incandescent lamp is…":"The BS symbol for a lamp is a circle with a cross inside. Learn the standard symbols so drawings are read the same way by everyone.","For an electrical fire the correct extinguisher to use is…":"CO₂ and dry powder do not conduct electricity. Water and foam extinguishers conduct and must never be used on live electrical fires.","Essential PPE for general electrical workshop testing includes…":"Dielectric boots, goggles and rated insulated gloves protect against shock and arcs. PPE must be suited to the voltage and kept in good condition.","Locking off and tagging (LOTO) the isolator prevents…":"A lock and tag on the isolator means nobody else can switch it on while you are working. It is a personal control.","Insulated hand tools (VDE certified to 1000V) are used to…":"Insulated tools rated to 1000 V reduce the chance of shock and of a tool bridging live parts and causing an arc.","The first action when you find a person in contact with live electrical equipment is to…":"Break contact safely without touching the person directly. Using dry non-conducting material or switching off removes the danger first.","Chest compressions and rescue breaths in CPR are given in a ratio of about…":"CPR is 30 chest compressions to 2 rescue breaths. The compressions keep blood circulating to the brain.","A safety sign that is a blue circle gives…":"A blue circle gives a mandatory instruction, something you must do. Red circles prohibit and yellow triangles warn.","A yellow triangular sign is used to show a…":"A yellow triangle warns of a hazard, such as electricity or slippery floors.","Burns from an electric shock should first be treated by…":"Cool a burn with clean running water for at least 10 minutes to stop the damage spreading. Do not use ice, creams or butter.","Why should a person who has received an electric shock be seen by a medical professional even if they feel well?":"Shock can disturb the heart rhythm and burn tissue inside the body. These effects can appear hours later, so always get checked.","PPE is considered the last line of defence because…":"Controls at the top of the hierarchy remove the hazard. PPE only protects the wearer after the others have failed, so it comes last.","Portable power tools on a building site are best supplied at…":"A 110 V centre-tapped supply gives only 55 V to earth, which is much less likely to kill. An RCD adds protection on 230 V tools.","When lifting a heavy load you should…":"Keep your back straight, bend your knees and hold the load close. This uses your leg muscles and protects your spine.","A tool used on electrical work should have handles that are…":"Insulated, undamaged handles protect against shock. Cracked or taped handles must be replaced.","PVC used for cable sheathing and core insulation is classified as an…":"PVC is an insulator. It covers the conductors and keeps the current inside them.","A primary engineering advantage of copper over aluminium is…":"Copper conducts better and is more ductile, so it bends and terminates more reliably than aluminium.","The insulation material inside fire-resistant MICC cable is…":"MICC cable is insulated with compacted magnesium oxide powder, which survives very high temperatures. That is why it is used for fire circuits.","The steel wire armour in an SWA cable provides…":"The armour protects against impact and can also act as the circuit protective conductor (CPC) in many installations.","Under current BS 7671 harmonised single-phase colour codes, the neutral conductor is…":"Under the harmonised colours, neutral is blue and live is brown. Green and yellow is earth.","18 mV/A/m cable carrying 16 A over a length of 20 m produces a voltage drop of…":"Voltage drop = 18 mV/A/m × 16 A × 20 m = 5,760 mV = 5.76 V. Divide millivolts by 1,000 to get volts.","Which factor does NOT affect the current-carrying capacity of a cable?":"The sheath colour has no effect on current capacity. Ambient temperature, grouping and insulation do.","The circuit protective conductor (CPC) in standard twin-and-earth cable must be…":"The bare CPC in twin-and-earth is sleeved green-and-yellow at every termination so everyone can identify it as the earth.","The older pre-harmonised UK/Ghana single-phase neutral conductor colour was…":"Before harmonisation the neutral was black and the live was red. Learn both colours for older installations.","The dedicated conductor that carries earth fault currents safely back to the supply is the…":"The CPC carries earth fault current safely back to the source, so the protective device can operate quickly.","A major installation challenge with aluminium conductors compared to copper is that aluminium…":"Aluminium oxidises and corrodes where it meets other metals. Use bimetallic or suitable terminals and prepare the joint carefully.","Flexible cords used on domestic portable appliances have stranded conductors in order to…":"Stranded conductors flex without breaking. Solid conductors would work-harden and crack from repeated bending.","The voltage drop along an electrical cable run increases directly with…":"Voltage drop rises with both the length of the run and the current drawn, because it is current times resistance.","A dry soldered joint is one that…":"A dry joint looks dull and grainy because the solder did not flow. It has high resistance and may fail or overheat.","The maximum continuous conductor temperature of standard PVC insulated cable is…":"Standard PVC insulation is rated for a conductor temperature of 70°C. Above that it softens and degrades.","Which cable has an armour layer that also acts as the circuit protective conductor?":"The steel wire armour acts as both mechanical protection and the CPC, so SWA is used for underground or exposed runs.","Which cable type is fire-resistant and used for emergency circuits?":"MICC is fire-resistant because both the conductor insulation and the sheath are inorganic. It continues to work in a fire.","Which of these REDUCES a cable's current-carrying capacity?":"Grouping cables and high ambient temperature both reduce how much heat can escape, so the cable must carry less current.","A typical minimum internal bending radius for small PVC cable is about…":"A minimum bend radius of about 4 times the cable diameter avoids damaging the insulation and conductors.","Flexible cord must NOT be used for…":"Flexible cord is for connecting appliances, not for fixed wiring in a building. Fixed wiring needs cable suited to being fixed.","When making a screw-terminal connection you should…":"Tighten to the correct torque with all strands in the terminal and no insulation trapped. A loose joint overheats.","Correction factors Ca, Cg and Ci stand for…":"Ca is the ambient temperature factor, Cg the grouping factor and Ci the thermal insulation factor.","The two checks a cable must pass after correction factors are applied are that Iz is at least…":"After correction factors, Iz must be at least In and Ib, so the cable can carry the protective device rating and the design current.","An MCB provides automatic protection against…":"An MCB has thermal and magnetic parts. The thermal part protects against overload and the magnetic part against short circuits.","An RCD operates on the engineering principle of…":"An RCD compares the current in the live and neutral. If some leaks to earth, the magnetic fields no longer balance and it trips.","For additional protection against electric shock, an RCD must have a rated residual operating current (IΔn) of…":"30 mA operating within 300 ms limits the shock to a level that is unlikely to be fatal. It is called additional protection.","Insulation resistance testing on a 230 V single-phase installation is carried out with…":"Insulation resistance on a 230 V circuit is tested at 500 V DC. The tester applies a high voltage to find leakage.","Dead tests on an electrical installation must be conducted…":"Dead tests are done before the circuit is energised, so there is no mains voltage present while testing.","The purpose of electrical polarity testing is to verify that…":"Polarity tests confirm that single-pole devices break the live conductor, so the equipment is dead when switched off.","Earthing of exposed conductive metalwork protects users by…":"A low-impedance earth path lets a large fault current flow so the protective device operates quickly, limiting the shock time.","An HRC fuse cartridge contains quartz sand in order to…":"The quartz sand quenches the arc and absorbs the energy when the fuse element melts in a short circuit.","The test button on an RCD is pressed by the user to check…":"The test button simulates a fault so you can check the mechanical operation of the RCD. Test it regularly.","A continuity test of a circuit protective conductor (CPC) is performed using…":"A low-resistance ohmmeter with at least 200 mA tests CPC continuity. A normal meter's current is too small to find poor joints.","An HRC (High Rupturing Capacity) cartridge fuse has an interrupt rating typically capable of safely clearing short circuits up to…":"An HRC fuse can safely clear very high fault currents, up to the figure marked on it, such as 80 kA, without rupturing.","The insulation resistance test between conductors and earth is categorized as a…":"Insulation resistance is a dead test. The supply must be isolated and locked off first because the tester applies voltage.","A type C MCB is most suitable for…":"A Type C MCB trips at a higher multiple of its rating, so it tolerates the inrush current of motors and inductive loads.","Which device compares the current in line and neutral conductors?":"An RCD measures the difference between the live and neutral currents. That difference is the earth leakage current.","A 30 mA RCD is used as additional protection mainly against…":"A 30 mA RCD protects mainly against electric shock from earth leakage, such as touching a faulty appliance.","Which fault is an RCD (not an RCBO) unable to protect against?":"A plain RCD does not protect against overload or L–N short circuits because those currents are balanced. An RCBO adds that protection.","Total earth fault loop impedance is calculated as…":"Zs is the external earth fault loop impedance Ze plus the circuit's own R1 + R2.","A final circuit on a TN system generally needs to disconnect within…":"For final circuits on a TN system the disconnection time is generally 0.4 seconds, to limit the shock duration.","A multimeter set to current mode must be connected…":"A multimeter measures current in series with the circuit. Connecting it across the supply on a current range creates a short circuit.","Before and after proving a circuit dead you should…":"Prove the voltage indicator on a known live source before and after testing, to be sure it still works.","The minimum test current of a low-resistance ohmmeter used for continuity is…":"A continuity tester should supply at least 200 mA, enough to find poor or high-resistance connections.","The prospective earth fault current in a circuit with U0 = 230 V and Zs = 0.46 Ω is…":"Fault current = U₀ ÷ Zs = 230 ÷ 0.46 = 500 A.","Controlling one lamp from two separate positions requires…":"Two two-way switches linked by two strappers let you control one lamp from two places.","To control a lamp from three or more separate positions, you must add…":"Intermediate switches are added between the two two-way switches to control the lamp from three or more positions.","A ring final circuit is characterised by the fact that…":"In a ring final circuit both ends of the live, neutral and CPC return to the same protective device, forming a ring.","A standard domestic ring final socket circuit uses…":"A domestic ring uses 2.5 mm² copper cable and a 32 A protective device.","The conduit space factor of 40% is mandated in regulations to…":"A conduit space factor of 40% leaves room for the cables to dissipate heat and to be drawn in without damage.","A steel fish tape (draw wire) is used in electrical installation to…":"A fish tape or draw wire is pushed through the conduit or trunking and used to pull the cables through.","A DOL starter for a three-phase motor incorporates…":"A DOL starter has a magnetic contactor to switch the motor and a thermal overload relay to protect it from overload.","A non-fused spur connected to a 32 A ring final circuit may supply a maximum of…":"A non-fused spur from a ring may feed only one single or one twin socket, so the spur cable is not overloaded.","An intermediate switch in a lighting installation is always wired between…":"An intermediate switch is wired between the two two-way switches in a multi-way lighting circuit.","A non-fused spur taken from a 32 A ring final circuit…":"A non-fused spur from a ring supplies a maximum of one single or twin socket outlet only.","The standard minimum conductor cross-sectional area for a domestic lighting circuit in copper is…":"Domestic lighting circuits typically use 1.0 or 1.5 mm² copper cable to carry the small lighting load.","The scale 1:50 on a drawing means that…":"A scale of 1:50 means 1 unit on the drawing equals 50 of the same units in the real building.","A drawing that shows how a circuit WORKS rather than where components are located is called a…":"A circuit diagram shows how a circuit works and how parts connect, not where they are in the building.","A drawing showing where sockets and lights are placed in a room is a…":"A layout drawing shows where sockets, lights and equipment are placed in the room.","The radius of a conduit bend should be at least…":"A bend radius of at least 2.5 times the conduit diameter stops the conduit kinking and lets cables be drawn in.","Two equal bends used to pass an obstruction form a…":"Two equal bends, one in each direction, form an offset that takes the conduit round an obstruction.","Joints in a junction box must be…":"Joints must be accessible so they can be inspected and maintained. Hidden joints cause hidden faults.","Why should grommets or bushes be fitted to metal boxes?":"Grommets or bushes protect cable insulation from the sharp edges of the knock-out hole.","A domestic cooker circuit is typically protected by…":"A cooker circuit is typically protected by a 32 A MCB.","On a two-way switch, the terminal that receives the live feed on Switch A is the…":"On a two-way switch the live feed goes to the common (COM) terminal on switch A.","The two strappers between two-way switches join…":"The strappers connect L1 to L1 and L2 to L2 between the two switches.","A domestic lighting circuit is commonly protected by…":"Domestic lighting circuits are commonly protected by a 6 A MCB.","Before installing from a drawing you should…":"Check that you have the latest drawing revision and report conflicts. Working from an old drawing causes costly mistakes.","A 230 V supply connected across a 46 Ω load draws a current of…":"I = V ÷ R = 230 ÷ 46 = 5 A.","Two 10 Ω resistors connected in parallel have an equivalent resistance of…":"Two equal resistors in parallel: R = 10 × 10 ÷ (10 + 10) = 5 Ω.","A 3 kW immersion heater operated continuously for 4 hours consumes…":"Energy = 3 kW × 4 h = 12 kWh.","The formula R = ρL ÷ A proves that the electrical resistance of a wire…":"R = ρL ÷ A: resistance grows with length and falls as the cross-sectional area grows.","Two 20 µF capacitors connected in series have a combined capacitance of…":"Capacitors in series add like resistors in parallel. Two 20 µF in series give 10 µF.","A single-phase transformer with 1,000 primary turns and 100 secondary turns on 230 V produces a secondary voltage of…":"Secondary voltage = 230 × (100 ÷ 1,000) = 23 V. The turns ratio sets the voltage ratio.","The RMS value of a sinusoidal AC current with a peak value of 10 A is approximately…":"RMS = peak ÷ √2 = 10 ÷ 1.414 = 7.07 A.","An electric current of 4 A flowing for 15 seconds transfers a charge of…":"Q = I × t = 4 A × 15 s = 60 C.","Kirchhoff's current law states that…":"Kirchhoff's current law: what flows into a junction equals what flows out, so current is not lost.","If 5 A and 3 A enter a junction and one branch takes 2 A away, the other branch carries…":"Total in = 5 + 3 = 8 A. One branch takes 2 A, so the other carries 8 − 2 = 6 A.","Kirchhoff's voltage law states that around a closed loop…":"Kirchhoff's voltage law: around a closed loop the voltage rises equal the voltage drops.","A 6 Ω resistor in series with a parallel pair of 12 Ω and 4 Ω has a total resistance of…":"12 Ω ∥ 4 Ω = 3 Ω. Add the 6 Ω in series: 6 + 3 = 9 Ω.","4.7 kΩ is equal to…":"The kilo prefix means 1,000, so 4.7 kΩ = 4,700 Ω.","250 mA expressed in amperes is…":"The milli prefix means one thousandth, so 250 mA = 0.25 A.","The unit of inductance is the…":"The unit of inductance is the henry (H).","Inductive reactance is calculated from…":"Inductive reactance X_L = 2πfL: it rises with frequency and with inductance.","A motor takes 3 kW input and delivers 2.4 kW output. Its efficiency is…":"Efficiency = output ÷ input × 100 = 2.4 ÷ 3 × 100 = 80%.","Power factor is defined as…":"Power factor = true power (kW) ÷ apparent power (kVA).","Electricity is stepped up to high voltage for long-distance transmission primarily to…":"Higher voltage means lower current for the same power, and loss in the line is I²R. So high voltage cuts heat loss in transmission.","In a balanced three-phase four-wire star system with a phase voltage of 230 V, the line voltage is…":"In a star system line voltage = phase voltage × √3 = 230 × 1.73 = 400 V.","Illuminance is scientifically defined as…":"Illuminance is luminous flux per unit area, measured in lux (lumens per square metre).","According to the Inverse Square Law of illumination, if the distance between a light source and a surface is doubled, the illuminance will…":"Light spreads over a larger area as distance grows, so doubling the distance cuts illuminance to one quarter.","Which of the following light sources offers the highest luminous efficacy (lumens per watt)?":"LED luminaires give the most lumens per watt of these sources.","The purpose of the choke (ballast) in a fluorescent luminaire during normal running is to…":"The choke limits and stabilises the arc current through the tube, which would otherwise rise without limit.","A room of 20 m² area illuminated by 4,000 lumens of light has an average illuminance of…":"Illuminance = lumens ÷ area = 4,000 ÷ 20 = 200 lux.","The power factor correction capacitor in a fluorescent luminaire is wired…":"The correction capacitor connects across the supply, in parallel, to correct the poor power factor of the choke.","A 2 kW heater used 3 hours a day for 30 days consumes…":"Energy = 2 kW × 3 h × 30 days = 180 kWh.","Energy in kilowatt-hours is calculated as…":"Energy in kWh = power in kW × time in hours.","Luminous efficacy is measured in…":"Efficacy is the light output per watt of electrical power, in lumens per watt.","Which lamp usually has the longest life and highest efficacy?":"LED lamps usually last longest and give the highest efficacy.","A lamp producing 3,000 lumens from 30 W has an efficacy of…":"Efficacy = 3,000 lm ÷ 30 W = 100 lm/W.","The lumen method uses MF to account for…":"The maintenance factor (MF) allows for dirt and ageing, so the lighting still meets the standard later in its life.","In the lumen method example, 12.5 luminaires are calculated. The sensible number to fit is…":"You cannot fit part of a luminaire, so round up to 13 to make sure the required light level is met.","Why should loads be spread across the three phases in a board?":"Spreading loads over the three phases balances the current and reduces the neutral current.","A feeder from a main board to a sub-board is called a…":"A feeder from the main board to a sub-board is called a submain.","Which tool describes the relationship between the main distribution board and a sub-distribution board?":"A board schedule or circuit chart lists each way, its device and what it supplies, and shows the link between boards.","The synchronous speed of a 4-pole three-phase induction motor operating on a 50 Hz supply is…":"Ns = 120 × f ÷ p = 120 × 50 ÷ 4 = 1,500 rpm.","To reverse the direction of rotation of a three-phase squirrel-cage induction motor, you must…":"Swapping any two of the three supply phases reverses the rotating field, so the motor turns the other way.","A fully charged lead-acid secondary battery cell produces an open-circuit voltage of approximately…":"A fully charged lead-acid cell is about 2.1 to 2.2 V.","An electrical hydrometer is used in battery maintenance to measure…":"A hydrometer measures the specific gravity of the electrolyte, which shows the state of charge.","An electrical ammeter must always be connected…":"An ammeter goes in series so the circuit current passes through it.","Transformer cores and machine armatures are constructed from thin laminations in order to…":"Thin laminations reduce eddy currents, so there is less heat and loss in cores and armatures.","A DC series motor is uniquely suited for traction and hoist applications because it possesses…":"A series motor has very high starting torque at low speed, so it suits traction and hoists.","Fleming's Left-Hand Rule is applied in electrical theory to determine the…":"Fleming's left-hand rule gives the direction of force on a current-carrying conductor, which is the motion in a motor.","The mechanical commutator in a DC generator performs the critical task of…":"The commutator reverses the coil connections every half turn, so the alternating voltage in the armature becomes one-directional DC at the brushes.","The specific gravity of a fully charged lead-acid battery electrolyte at standard temperature is…":"A fully charged lead-acid cell has an electrolyte specific gravity of about 1.280. A lower reading shows it is discharged.","Which material is used for permanent magnets?":"Permanent magnets need hard magnetic material such as steel, which keeps its magnetism. Soft iron loses it when the current stops.","Increasing the turns on a solenoid…":"More turns on a solenoid give more ampere-turns, so the magnetic field is stronger.","A 400/690 V motor connected to a 400 V three-phase supply is connected in…":"A 400/690 V motor on a 400 V supply must be connected in delta so each winding gets its rated 400 V.","Direct-on-line starting current of an induction motor is typically…":"An induction motor draws a starting current of about 5 to 8 times its full-load current when started direct on line.","The overload relay on a motor starter is set to…":"The overload relay is set to the full-load current on the motor nameplate so it trips on a sustained overload but not on normal running.","A 100 Ah battery supplying 5 A would theoretically last…":"Time = capacity ÷ current = 100 Ah ÷ 5 A = 20 hours, in theory. Real batteries last a little less.","Connecting batteries in series results in…":"Cells in series add their voltages. The capacity in amp-hours stays the same.","Why should battery rooms be ventilated?":"Charging gives off hydrogen, which is explosive. Ventilation stops it collecting to a dangerous level.","Which instrument measures current without breaking the circuit?":"A clamp meter measures the magnetic field around a conductor, so you do not need to break the circuit.","Reading a meter scale straight on avoids…":"Look straight at the scale so the pointer and its reflection line up. Reading from the side gives a parallax error.","In a TT earthing system, the consumer's protective earth conductor (CPC) is connected to…":"In a TT system the consumer's earth is a local electrode, independent of the supply neutral. This is why RCDs are needed.","Circuits operating at Extra-Low Voltage (fire alarms, data) and Low Voltage (230V power) in trunking must be…":"ELV and LV circuits in trunking must be separated by a metallic divider unless every cable is insulated for the highest voltage present.","Cut ends of MICC (mineral-insulated) cable must be sealed immediately with a brass pot and compound because…":"MICC insulation absorbs moisture from the air quickly. Seal the cut end at once with a pot and compound.","The maximum allowable trunking space factor under standard wiring regulations is…":"The space factor for trunking is 45% of the internal cross-section. Leaving space lets cables dissipate heat and be drawn in.","Surrounding an earth rod pit with charcoal dust and salt serves to…":"Charcoal and salt keep the soil damp and conductive around the rod, which lowers its resistance.","In an electrical installation with a measured loop impedance Zs of 0.5 Ω on a 230 V supply, the prospective earth fault current is…":"Prospective fault current = 230 ÷ 0.5 = 460 A. The lower the loop impedance, the greater the fault current and the faster the device trips.","A modern current-operated RCD has superseded older voltage-operated ELCBs because…":"An RCD senses the actual leakage current, whatever path it takes. Voltage-operated ELCBs could be fooled by parallel earth paths.","In a TN-C-S earthing system, the supply neutral and earth conductors are…":"In TN-C-S the supply neutral and earth are one conductor (PEN) in the street. They are separated at the intake into neutral and earth.","Which cables may share the same trunking compartment?":"Cables sharing a trunking compartment must all be insulated for the highest voltage present, so a fault cannot reach the lower-voltage circuit.","A metal motor frame that could become live under fault is a…":"An exposed-conductive-part is metalwork of the equipment, such as a motor frame, that could become live in a fault.","An incoming metal water pipe is bonded to the main earthing terminal. The pipe is classed as a…":"An extraneous-conductive-part is not part of the electrical installation but could carry a potential, such as a metal water pipe. It is bonded to earth.","The generally accepted minimum size of a copper main protective bonding conductor is…":"The generally accepted minimum size for a copper main protective bonding conductor is 6 mm².","The maximum earth electrode resistance with a 30 mA RCD on a TT system (Ra × I∆n ≤ 50 V) is…":"Ra ≤ 50 V ÷ 0.03 A = 1,667 Ω. The electrode must be low enough that the touch voltage on a fault stays below 50 V.","The maximum disconnection time for final circuits up to 32 A on a TN system is…":"On a TN system the usual maximum disconnection time for final circuits up to 32 A is 0.4 s.","The maximum disconnection time for final circuits up to 32 A on a TT system is…":"On a TT system the maximum disconnection time for final circuits up to 32 A is 0.2 s.","Which method lowers the resistance of an earth electrode?":"A longer rod, or several rods spaced apart, gives more contact with the soil and so lowers resistance.","Why is an RCD required on a TT system?":"On a TT system the earth fault loop impedance is high, so the fault current is too small for an MCB or fuse. An RCD detects the leakage and trips.","Cable tray is preferred for…":"Cable tray supports heavy armoured and multicore cables, and its open design lets heat escape.","Fire alarm cables are segregated from other circuits so that…":"Alarm cables are segregated so a fault in power circuits cannot disable the alarm.","Supplementary bonding is used…":"Supplementary bonding links exposed and extraneous parts within a location, such as a bathroom, where there is extra shock risk.","A single-phase transformer has 600 primary turns and 60 secondary turns. If the primary voltage is 240 V, the secondary voltage is…":"Vs = 240 × 60 ÷ 600 = 24 V. The voltage ratio equals the turns ratio.","Transformers and AC alternators are rated in kVA rather than kW because…":"Core losses depend on voltage and copper losses on current, neither on the power factor, so these machines are rated in kVA.","A 6-pole three-phase induction motor operating on a 50 Hz supply has a synchronous speed of…":"Ns = 120 × 50 ÷ 6 = 1,000 rpm.","If a 4-pole 50 Hz induction motor with a synchronous speed of 1,500 rpm operates under load at 1,425 rpm, its percentage slip is…":"Slip = (1,500 − 1,425) ÷ 1,500 × 100 = 5%.","A Star-Delta starter reduces the starting current drawn by a three-phase motor to approximately…":"Star-delta starting puts each winding on a lower voltage at first, so the starting current falls to about one-third of DOL.","In a forward-and-reverse motor starter, electrical and mechanical interlocks are installed to…":"Interlocks stop both contactors closing together, which would short two phases together.","The no-volt release feature in an industrial motor starter control circuit ensures that…":"No-volt release drops the starter out when power fails, so the motor does not restart suddenly and injure someone when power returns.","To reverse the direction of rotation of a single-phase capacitor-start induction motor, you must…":"Reversing either the start winding or the run winding connections reverses the field and so the direction of rotation.","A transformer has 1,000 primary turns and 104 secondary turns. With 230 V on the primary, the secondary voltage is about…":"Vs = 230 × 104 ÷ 1,000 = 23.9 V, about 24 V.","Iron losses in a transformer are…":"Iron losses depend on the flux, which depends on the voltage. The voltage is almost constant, so these losses are constant.","Copper losses in a transformer vary with…":"Copper losses are I²R, so they vary with the square of the load current.","A transformer's efficiency is greatest when…":"A transformer's efficiency is greatest when the copper losses equal the iron losses.","The secondary of a current transformer must never be…":"An open-circuited CT secondary can produce a dangerously high voltage. Short it before disconnecting the instrument.","The synchronous speed of an 8-pole motor on 50 Hz is…":"Ns = 120 × 50 ÷ 8 = 750 rpm.","A 4-pole motor running at 1,440 rpm on 50 Hz has a slip of…":"Slip = (1,500 − 1,440) ÷ 1,500 × 100 = 4%.","Star-delta starting reduces the starting current to about…":"Star-delta starting reduces the starting current to about one-third of the DOL value.","Which starter uses thyristors to ramp up voltage smoothly?":"A soft starter uses thyristors to raise the voltage smoothly and so limits starting current and shock to the load.","A variable speed drive controls motor speed by varying…":"A variable speed drive changes the supply frequency (and voltage), which sets the motor's synchronous speed.","Which single-phase motor has the highest starting torque?":"A capacitor-start motor has the highest starting torque of the common single-phase types.","The no-volt release in a starter prevents…":"No-volt release prevents the motor restarting by itself after a supply failure.","In a balanced star-connected three-phase system, the relationship between line and phase voltage is…":"In a star system line voltage = √3 × phase voltage, so 230 V phase gives 400 V line.","In a balanced delta-connected three-phase system, the line current is…":"In a delta system line current = √3 × phase current.","The total active power consumed by a balanced 400 V three-phase motor drawing 10 A at a power factor of 0.8 is…":"P = √3 × V × I × cos φ = 1.732 × 400 × 10 × 0.8 = 5.54 kW.","A circuit consisting of a 30 Ω resistor and a 40 Ω inductive reactance in series has a total impedance Z of…":"Z = √(R² + X²) = √(30² + 40²) = √2,500 = 50 Ω.","Power factor (cos φ) in an AC electrical installation is defined as the ratio of…":"Power factor = true power (kW) ÷ apparent power (kVA).","A low lagging power factor in an industrial plant results in…":"A low power factor means higher line current for the same real power, so more cable heating and often a utility penalty.","The standard engineering method for improving the lagging power factor of an induction motor installation is to…":"Power factor correction capacitors connected in parallel supply the reactive current locally, so the line carries less.","The dangerous stroboscopic effect where spinning lathe chucks appear stationary under discharge lighting is eliminated by…":"Connecting adjacent luminaires to different phases means the light never dips to zero at the same time, which removes the stroboscopic effect.","A three-phase load draws 20 A from 400 V at a power factor of 0.8. The real power is about…":"P = √3 × 400 × 20 × 0.8 = 11.1 kW.","The neutral current in a perfectly balanced three-phase load is…":"With a balanced load the three phase currents cancel at the star point, so the neutral current is zero.","The peak voltage of a 230 V RMS sine wave is about…":"Peak = RMS × √2 = 230 × 1.414 = 325 V.","The period of a 50 Hz supply is…":"Period = 1 ÷ frequency = 1 ÷ 50 = 0.02 s, or 20 ms.","In a purely capacitive AC circuit the current…":"In a capacitive circuit the current reaches its peak a quarter cycle before the voltage, so it leads by 90°.","The inductive reactance of a 0.1 H coil at 50 Hz is about…":"X_L = 2πfL = 2 × 3.142 × 50 × 0.1 = 31.4 Ω.","A series circuit has R = 10 Ω, XL = 30 Ω and XC = 20 Ω. Its impedance is…":"Net reactance = 30 − 20 = 10 Ω. Z = √(10² + 10²) = 14.1 Ω.","At series resonance…":"At series resonance X_L equals X_C, so they cancel and the impedance is just R.","The average value of a sine wave over a half cycle is…":"The average value of a sine wave over a half cycle is 0.637 times the peak.","Power factor correction capacitors are connected…":"Correction capacitors go in parallel with the load, so they do not interrupt the load current.","A 50 kW load improved from 0.7 to 0.95 power factor needs about…":"Reactive power needed = 50 × (tan φ₁ − tan φ₂) = 50 × (1.02 − 0.33) ≈ 34.6 kVAr.","A risk of over-correcting the power factor is…":"Over-correcting gives a leading power factor, which can raise the voltage and cause resonance with the supply.","A non-maintained emergency luminaire is designed to…":"A non-maintained emergency luminaire lights only when the normal mains fails.","In an intruder or fire alarm system, a closed-circuit loop is preferred because…":"A closed-circuit loop is fail-safe. Cutting a wire opens the loop and triggers the alarm.","In the Lumen Method formula N = (E × A) ÷ (Φ × UF × MF), the Maintenance Factor (MF) accounts for…":"The maintenance factor allows for lamps losing output as they age and for dirt on the fittings.","A workshop requiring 24,000 total lumens on the working plane where each fitting provides 2,000 effective lumens needs…":"N = 24,000 ÷ 2,000 = 12 luminaires.","When selecting a cable size for an installation, the protective device rating In must satisfy…":"Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz: the device rating must be at least the design current but not more than the cable's capacity.","The design current Ib for a 7.2 kW electric shower operating on a 230 V single-phase supply is approximately…":"Ib = P ÷ V = 7,200 ÷ 230 = 31.3 A.","Cables dedicated to fire alarm systems (e.g. FP200 or MICC) must be…":"Fire alarm cables must be fire-resistant and separated from power cables so a power fault cannot disable the alarm.","A luminaire reflector serves the primary optical function of…":"A reflector directs light on to the working plane and so makes the fitting more efficient.","A non-maintained emergency luminaire lights…":"A non-maintained luminaire lights only when the normal supply fails.","The usual minimum rated duration of emergency lighting is…":"Emergency lighting is usually required to last at least 3 hours.","The minimum illuminance on the centre line of an escape route is…":"The minimum illuminance on the centre line of an escape route is 1 lux.","Which detector is best for smouldering fires?":"Optical smoke detectors respond best to slow, smouldering fires with large smoke particles.","Where would a heat detector be used instead of a smoke detector?":"A heat detector avoids false alarms from cooking steam and smoke in places like kitchens.","A sounder in a fire alarm system should be at least…":"A sounder should be at least 65 dB(A), or 5 dB above any background noise, so that it is heard.","The voltage drop limit for a lighting circuit on 230 V is about…":"The usual limit for lighting is 3% of 230 V, which is 6.9 V.","The voltage drop for 18 mV/A/m, 20 A and 25 m is…":"Voltage drop = 18 × 20 × 25 ÷ 1,000 = 9.0 V.","PIR detectors respond to…":"A PIR detector senses the change in infrared heat when a warm body moves across its field.","A tamper loop is used to…":"A tamper loop protects the enclosure and cables, and triggers the alarm if anyone interferes with them.","Why is a closed-circuit alarm loop better than an open-circuit one?":"A closed-circuit loop triggers the alarm if a cable is cut or a contact opens. An open-circuit loop would stay silent.","An emergency lighting system should be tested…":"Emergency lighting must be tested regularly, with a monthly function test and an annual full-duration test, and the results logged.","An LDR (Light-Dependent Resistor) exposed to total darkness exhibits…":"An LDR in the dark has very high resistance, often around 1 MΩ. Light lowers its resistance.","In an AND logic gate with inputs A and B, the output is 1 (HIGH) if and only if…":"An AND gate output is 1 only when both inputs are 1.","A NAND logic gate with both inputs set to logic 1 produces an output of…":"A NAND gate is an AND followed by NOT. With both inputs at 1 the output is 0.","An EXOR (Exclusive-OR) logic gate produces an output of 1 when…":"An exclusive-OR gate gives 1 when the two inputs differ.","A full-wave bridge rectifier circuit converts AC to pulsating DC using…":"A bridge rectifier uses four diodes in a diamond so that both halves of the AC wave produce current in the same direction.","The correct functional order of blocks in a regulated DC power supply is…":"The usual order is transformer, rectifier, smoothing filter, voltage regulator, then the load.","A Zener diode is operated in reverse breakdown mode primarily to perform…":"A Zener diode in reverse breakdown holds a steady voltage, so it is used for regulation and reference.","A Triac is widely used in domestic AC light dimmers and ceiling fan controllers because it…":"A triac conducts in both directions, so it can control both halves of an AC wave in dimmers and fan controllers.","A 5-metre long workshop wall drawn on a blueprint at a scale of 1:50 measures…":"500 cm ÷ 50 = 10 cm on the drawing.","Balancing single-phase loads evenly across L1, L2, and L3 on a three-phase distribution board is essential to…":"Balancing loads across the three phases keeps the neutral current low and avoids overheating the neutral.","A resistor coloured brown, black, red, gold is…":"Brown = 1, black = 0, red = ×100, gold = ±5%. So 1,000 Ω = 1 kΩ ±5%.","A resistor coloured yellow, violet, orange is…":"Yellow = 4, violet = 7, orange = ×1,000. So 47,000 Ω = 47 kΩ.","The silver tolerance band on a resistor means…":"A silver band means a tolerance of ±10%, and gold means ±5%.","A 12 V supply drives a 2 V, 20 mA LED. The series resistor should be…":"R = (12 − 2) V ÷ 0.02 A = 500 Ω.","A flywheel diode across a relay coil protects the transistor from…":"When a relay coil is switched off its collapsing field produces a voltage spike (back-EMF). The flywheel diode absorbs it and protects the transistor.","A thyristor, once triggered, turns off when…":"A thyristor stays on after it is triggered, and turns off only when the current falls below its holding level, usually at zero in AC.","Which gate is called a universal gate?":"NAND is called the universal gate because any other logic function can be built from NAND gates alone.","Binary 1011 in decimal is…":"Binary 1011 = 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11.","Decimal 13 in binary is…":"Decimal 13 = 8 + 4 + 1 = 1101 in binary.","A + A' in Boolean algebra equals…":"A OR NOT A is always true, so A + A' = 1.","A connected load of 40 kW with a diversity factor of 0.6 gives a maximum demand of…":"Maximum demand = 40 kW × 0.6 = 24 kW. Not every load runs at once.","Wastage allowance on a cable estimate is normally…":"Allow 5 to 10% extra cable for waste, bends and errors.","A classroom wall measuring 8.5 metres in actual length will measure how many centimetres on a 1:100 scale blueprint?":"850 cm ÷ 100 = 8.5 cm on the drawing.","On an architectural trade drawing, the door swing arc indicates…":"The door swing arc shows which way the door opens and the clearance needed around it.","In electrical terminology, a TPN distribution board stands for…":"TPN stands for three-phase and neutral. A TPN board feeds three-phase circuits with a neutral.","In a Star-Delta motor starter power circuit, the two contactors that must be interlocked are…":"The star and delta contactors are interlocked so they can never be on together, which would short the supply.","A forward-and-reverse motor starter reverses the direction of three-phase rotation by…":"Swapping two of the three incoming phases reverses the motor's rotating field and its direction.","The timer relay inside an automatic Star-Delta starter performs the task of…":"The timer drops out the star contactor and pulls in the delta contactor after the motor has run up to speed.","In a TPN distribution board, single-phase 230 V circuits are deliberately distributed across L1, L2, and L3 in order to…":"Spreading single-phase circuits over L1, L2 and L3 balances the load, so the neutral current is small and the supply is used evenly.","On an electrical layout floor plan, tick marks drawn across a conduit line indicate…":"Tick marks across a conduit line show how many conductors run in it, so the installer knows what to draw in.","The dimensions of A4 paper are…":"A4 paper is 210 × 297 mm.","Which paper size is 297 × 420 mm?":"A3 paper measures 297 × 420 mm. It is twice the size of A4.","A 45 mm line on a 1:50 drawing represents a real length of…":"45 mm × 50 = 2,250 mm = 2.25 m.","A 12 m wall drawn at 1:200 measures…":"12 m = 12,000 mm. 12,000 ÷ 200 = 60 mm on the drawing.","A dashed line on a drawing normally shows…":"A dashed line shows hidden detail, such as something behind or below the surface.","The title block of a drawing includes…":"The title block gives the drawing number, scale, date and revision, so you know which drawing you hold and whether it is the latest.","A drawing that shows how a control circuit works with rungs between supply rails is a…":"A ladder diagram draws the control circuit as rungs between two supply rails, so it is easy to follow how it works.","An as-built drawing shows…":"An as-built drawing records what was actually installed, including any changes made during the job.","In a ladder diagram contacts are drawn in the…":"Contacts in a ladder diagram are drawn in their de-energised (normal) state, with no coil energised and no one pressing a button.","In a motor control rung, the stop button (NC) is placed…":"The NC stop button goes in series, first in the rung, so pressing it breaks the supply to the whole rung.","A cable schedule lists…":"A cable schedule lists the cable size, type, length and route for each circuit.","After a drawing is enlarged or reduced on a photocopier you should…":"Enlarging or reducing a drawing changes its scale, so always use the written dimensions and not a ruler.","In the DC generator EMF equation E = (Φ Z N P) ÷ (60 A), the value of A for a wave-wound armature is…":"For a wave-wound armature there are always 2 parallel paths, whatever the number of poles, so A = 2.","In a 4-pole lap-wound DC generator, the number of parallel armature current paths A is equal to…":"In a lap-wound armature the number of parallel paths equals the number of poles, so a 4-pole machine has A = 4.","If the shunt field circuit of a running DC shunt motor is accidentally opened on no-load, the motor will…":"With no field flux the back-EMF stays low, so the armature current rises and the speed climbs very high. The motor can destroy itself.","A self-excited DC shunt generator fails to build up output voltage when spun up to rated speed. The most probable root cause is…":"If the pole cores lose their residual magnetism, there is no small starting flux to build the voltage up, so the generator fails to excite.","Excessive arcing and sparking at the commutator brushes of a DC motor is commonly caused by…":"Worn brushes, weak springs or high mica cause poor contact and sparking at the commutator.","When laying an underground armoured cable run, high-visibility warning tape is installed…":"Warning tape goes part-way up the trench, so anyone digging finds it before they reach the cable.","Underground electrical cables are bedded in a layer of stone-free sieved sand in order to…":"Stone-free sand prevents sharp stones from damaging the cable sheath as the ground settles.","Back-EMF (Eb) generated in the armature of a running DC motor serves to…":"The back-EMF opposes the supply voltage and so limits the armature current to a safe value as the motor speeds up.","The back-EMF of a DC motor is calculated as…":"The armature current is Ia = (V − Eb) ÷ Ra, so Eb = V − Ia × Ra.","A motor on 230 V with Ia = 20 A and Ra = 0.5 Ω has a back-EMF of…":"Eb = 230 − (20 × 0.5) = 220 V.","The starting current of the same motor with no starter (230 V, Ra = 0.5 Ω) would be…":"At standstill there is no back-EMF, so I = V ÷ Ra = 230 ÷ 0.5 = 460 A. That is why a starter is needed.","The purpose of a DC motor starter is to…":"A starter adds series resistance to limit the large starting current until the back-EMF has built up.","Weakening the field of a DC shunt motor…":"A weaker field means lower back-EMF at a given speed, so the motor speeds up until the back-EMF balances again.","To reverse a DC motor you should reverse…":"Reversing either the armature or the field connections reverses the direction. If you reverse both, it turns the same way.","A DC series motor must never be run…":"A series motor has no load limit on its speed, so with no load it can race dangerously. It must always be connected to a load.","The no-volt release coil in a three-point starter is wired in series with the…":"The no-volt release coil is in series with the shunt field, so loss of supply releases the starter handle to off.","Armature reaction and sparking are reduced by fitting…":"Interpoles (commutating poles) cancel the effect of armature reaction at the commutator, reducing sparking.","A motor takes 5 kW and delivers 4.3 kW. Its efficiency is…":"Efficiency = 4.3 ÷ 5 × 100 = 86%.","A healthy commutator surface should be…":"A healthy commutator is smooth and has a polished brown film. Grooves, burns or black marks show a fault.","Which test checks the insulation of motor windings to the frame?":"An insulation resistance test with a megger finds weak insulation between the windings and the frame.","Under IEC 60364 and BS 7671, the standard method of protecting against indirect electric shock is…":"ADS combines earthing, bonding and fast protective devices so a fault is cleared quickly and cannot hold a dangerous voltage.","BS 7671 specifies a maximum automatic disconnection time for 230 V final socket circuits in TN systems of…":"For 230 V final socket circuits on TN systems, the maximum disconnection time is 0.4 seconds.","Under the IEC 60529 Ingress Protection system, an enclosure rated IP65 is certified as…":"IP65 means completely dust-tight (6) and protected against water jets (5).","In the IP rating code (e.g. IP54), the first digit and second digit represent protection against…":"The first digit is protection against solids and dust, and the second digit is protection against water.","Electrical test probes used for verifying dead circuits must comply with GS 38 by incorporating…":"GS 38 probes have finger guards and shielded tips, so a slipped probe cannot short a live part, and are used with fused leads.","In a TN-C-S (PME) electrical supply system, the neutral and protective earth conductors are…":"In TN-C-S the neutral and earth are combined as a PEN conductor in the street supply and split into separate neutral and earth at the intake.","A Permit-to-Work (PTW) is a formal written health and safety document used in industry to…":"A permit to work is a formal written authority that makes sure the circuit is isolated, locked off and proved dead before hazardous work begins.","In a TT earthing system, why is an RCD mandatory for fault protection rather than an ordinary MCB?":"On a TT supply the earth rod's resistance is high, so the fault current is too small for an MCB. An RCD senses the leakage and trips.","The relationship between design current Ib, device rating In and cable capacity Iz must be…":"The cable must carry at least the protective device rating, and the device must carry the design current: Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz.","Additional protection for socket outlets up to 32 A is given by…":"A 30 mA RCD gives additional protection for sockets up to 32 A, mainly because those are used for portable appliances outdoors.","The breaking capacity of a protective device must be at least…":"A protective device must be able to break the largest fault current that can occur where it is fitted, or it may fail dangerously.","A surge protective device (SPD) is used to…":"A surge protective device diverts and limits short, high voltage spikes from lightning or switching, so equipment is not damaged.","Bathroom Zone 1 is…":"Zone 1 is the space above the bath or shower tray up to 2.25 m. It has strict limits on what can be installed.","Which of the following is NOT normally allowed in bathroom zones 0, 1 and 2?":"A standard 13 A socket is not allowed in the bathroom zones, because water and electric shock are a serious combination.","An arc fault detection device (AFDD) detects…":"An AFDD detects dangerous arcing in a final circuit and disconnects it, reducing fire risk from damaged wiring.","Cables on escape routes should be…":"Low smoke and fume cable gives off little toxic smoke in a fire, which protects people escaping along the route.","The document produced after periodic inspection is an…":"An Electrical Installation Condition Report is issued after periodic inspection and testing.","Domestic rented property is typically inspected every…":"A five-year inspection interval is typical for rented domestic property.","Fire stops are used to…":"Fire stopping seals the holes cables pass through, so fire and smoke cannot spread through walls and floors.","Undervoltage protection on a motor circuit prevents…":"Undervoltage protection stops a motor restarting by itself after a supply failure, which could injure someone working on the machine.","The minimum acceptable insulation resistance for a newly installed 230 V single-phase domestic installation tested at 500 V DC is…":"A new 230 V installation should read at least 1.0 MΩ at 500 V DC. Anything lower shows damage or moisture.","In the ring final continuity test, cross-connecting the line and neutral ends of the loop (L1 to N2 and L2 to N1) allows the technician to…":"Cross-connecting the loop makes the reading the same at every socket. A high or low reading shows an open circuit, a spur or a bridge.","Earth fault loop impedance Zs is mathematically related to external impedance Ze and circuit resistance by…":"Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2): the external impedance plus the circuit's own resistance of line and cpc.","When tested with a calibrated instrument at its rated residual operating current (1 × IΔn), a 30 mA RCD must trip within…":"A 30 mA RCD must trip within 300 ms at its rated current (1 × IΔn).","When cables are installed in a hot tropical environment with ambient temperature of 40°C, the current rating of the cable must be…":"At 40°C the cable cannot lose heat as easily, so its current rating must be reduced by the correction factor Ca.","If an electrical circuit is protected by a BS 3036 semi-enclosed rewirable fuse, what correction factor Cc must be applied to the cable sizing equation?":"A semi-enclosed rewirable fuse is less accurate, so the cable rating must be multiplied by 0.725 to keep a safe margin.","A 9.2 kW load operating on a 230 V single-phase supply draws a design current Ib of…":"Ib = P ÷ V = 9,200 ÷ 230 = 40 A.","During initial verification, what must be done with electronic equipment (such as dimmer switches, computers and LED drivers) before performing the 500 V DC insulation test?":"Dimmers, computers and LED drivers can be damaged by 500 V DC, so disconnect them or link their terminals before testing.","The approximate resistance per metre of 2.5 mm² twin and earth (1.5 mm² cpc) is…":"For 2.5 mm² line and 1.5 mm² cpc, R1 + R2 is about 19.5 mΩ per metre (line 7.41 plus cpc 12.1).","For a 20 m run of 2.5/1.5 mm² cable, R1 + R2 is about…":"R1 + R2 = 20 m × 19.5 mΩ/m = 390 mΩ = 0.39 Ω.","If Ze = 0.35 Ω and R1 + R2 = 0.39 Ω, the earth fault loop impedance Zs is…":"Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2) = 0.35 + 0.39 = 0.74 Ω.","The minimum insulation resistance at 500 V DC for LV circuits is…":"Low voltage circuits must read at least 1 MΩ at 500 V DC.","SELV and PELV circuits are tested at…":"SELV and PELV circuits are tested at 250 V DC and must read at least 0.5 MΩ.","In a ring final circuit, r2 (cpc) is about… times r1 for 2.5/1.5 mm² cable.":"The cpc in 2.5/1.5 cable is thinner than the line conductor, so its resistance is higher. The ratio is roughly 1.6 to 1.7 times.","When the ring is cross-connected line to neutral, readings at every socket should be…":"With line and neutral cross-connected, the reading at each socket is about (r1 + rn) ÷ 4, the same at all of them.","A higher reading at one socket during ring testing suggests…":"A higher reading at one socket suggests a spur from that socket or a loose connection.","An RCD must trip within… at its rated residual current.":"An RCD must trip within 300 ms at its rated residual current.","An RCD must trip within… at five times its rated residual current.":"At five times its rated residual current an RCD must trip within 40 ms.","Which certificate is issued for a new installation?":"A new installation gets an Electrical Installation Certificate (EIC).","A 6 kW load at 230 V takes a design current of about…":"Ib = 6,000 ÷ 230 = 26.1 A, about 26 A.","In a solar photovoltaic (PV) array, connecting solar panels in series strings serves to…":"Series strings add the module voltages. The current stays the same as one module.","Why is an MPPT charge controller significantly superior to a standard PWM controller?":"MPPT keeps the panel at its maximum power point and turns extra voltage into extra charging current, so it gives more yield than PWM.","A pure sine wave inverter is strictly required for inductive loads (such as refrigerators, air conditioners, and water pumps) because…":"Modified sine waves make motors run hot, noisy and shorten the life of their windings, so inductive loads need a pure sine inverter.","The safety function known as \"anti-islanding\" in a grid-tied solar inverter ensures that…":"Anti-islanding disconnects the inverter when the grid fails, so linesmen are not electrocuted by power fed back into the network.","When designing a solar battery storage bank using deep-cycle lead-acid batteries (AGM/Gel), the maximum recommended Depth of Discharge (DoD) to avoid premature sulfation is…":"Deep discharge of lead-acid batteries causes sulfation. Limiting the depth of discharge to about 50% protects their life.","Why must standard AC miniature circuit breakers (MCBs) NEVER be installed on high-voltage DC solar circuits?":"DC has no natural zero crossing, so a DC arc does not go out by itself. Use breakers rated for DC.","The open-circuit voltage (Voc) of a solar PV module is defined as…":"Open-circuit voltage is the module voltage with no load connected (current = 0). It is the highest voltage the panel produces.","In an off-grid solar system, the correct and safe sequence for connecting the charge controller is…":"Connect the battery first so the controller starts and sets its voltage range, then connect the solar array. Disconnect in reverse order.","Compared to traditional lead-acid batteries, Lithium Iron Phosphate (LiFePO4) solar batteries offer…":"LiFePO4 batteries allow a deep discharge of 80 to 90% and many more charge cycles than lead-acid batteries.","All exposed metallic mounting rails and frames of a rooftop solar array must be…":"All metal rails and frames must be bonded and connected to the main earth terminal so no part can become live and dangerous.","A home uses 3 kWh a day with 5 sun hours and 80% system efficiency. The array size needed is about…":"Array size = daily energy ÷ (sun hours × efficiency) = 3 ÷ (5 × 0.8) = 0.75 kWp.","For 6 kWh usable energy from lead-acid at 50% depth of discharge, the nominal battery capacity is…":"At 50% depth of discharge you can use half the nominal capacity, so 6 kWh usable needs 12 kWh nominal.","A 12 kWh battery at 48 V has a capacity of…":"Ah = Wh ÷ V = 12,000 ÷ 48 = 250 Ah.","Modules connected in series give…":"Series connection adds voltage and keeps the current the same.","Four 40 V, 10 A modules in series have a string output of…":"Voltage = 4 × 40 = 160 V. The current stays at 10 A.","The highest open-circuit voltage of a string occurs on…":"Module voltage rises as it gets colder, so the highest open-circuit voltage occurs on the coldest day.","Conductor and protective device ratings in PV circuits are commonly based on…":"PV circuit conductors and protective devices are commonly sized at 1.25 times the short-circuit current.","Standard test conditions (STC) for PV modules include an irradiance of…":"STC uses an irradiance of 1,000 W/m² (with a 25°C cell temperature).","DC arcs are more dangerous than AC arcs because…":"DC has no zero crossing, so an arc does not extinguish itself and can burn continuously.","Modules in Ghana should be tilted at least…":"A tilt of at least about 10° lets rain wash the dust off the modules.","Anti-islanding in a grid-tied inverter means…":"Anti-islanding means the inverter shuts down when the grid supply fails, to protect people working on the lines.","Which system keeps critical loads running during a power cut and also connects to the grid?":"A hybrid system connects to the grid and uses a battery, so critical loads keep running in a power cut.","A formal business quotation letter addressed to \"Dear Sir\" should conclude with which professional sign-off?":"A formal letter beginning Dear Sir ends with Yours faithfully.","If an electrical contractor purchases GH₵ 5,000 worth of materials and adds a 15% profit margin, the total charged to the client for materials is…":"15% of 5,000 = 750, so the total is 5,000 + 750 = GH₵ 5,750.","An electrical installation workshop measuring 6 metres wide by 12 metres long has a total floor area of…":"Area = width × length = 6 × 12 = 72 m².","An electrical appliance rated at 3.5 kW operating on a 230 V supply has a power consumption in Watts of…":"3.5 kW = 3,500 W. Multiply kilowatts by 1,000 to get watts.","What is the crucial commercial difference between an estimate and a formal quotation?":"A quotation is a fixed price offer, often binding. An estimate is an approximate price that can change.","A customer invoice issued by an electrical contractor serves the primary function of…":"An invoice requests payment for work done or milestones reached, under the agreed terms.","When ordering 2.5 mm² cable, an experienced contractor measures 80 metres on the blueprint and adds 10% for drops, bends, and terminations. The total length ordered is…":"Allow 10% extra: 80 × 0.1 = 8 m, so order 80 + 8 = 88 m.","Which professional practice best builds long-term reputation and client trust for an electrician?":"Punctuality, genuine materials, tidy work and written quotes and receipts build trust and a good reputation.","A formal letter beginning \"Dear Sir\" is ended with…":"A letter beginning Dear Sir ends with Yours faithfully.","A letter beginning \"Dear Mr Mensah\" is ended with…":"A letter beginning with a name, such as Dear Mr Mensah, ends with Yours sincerely.","The correct order of parts of a report is…":"A report is usually ordered as title, introduction, findings, conclusion and recommendations.","15% of 800 is…":"15% of 800 = 0.15 × 800 = 120.","Adding 15% profit to a cost of 960 gives…":"15% of 960 = 144, so the price is 960 + 144 = 1,104.","A 10% discount on 1,200 gives…":"10% of 1,200 = 120, so 1,200 − 120 = 1,080.","Mixing sand and cement in the ratio 3:1 for 20 kg total gives sand of…":"Sand is 3 parts of 4, so 3 ÷ 4 × 20 = 15 kg.","A worker earns 25 per hour and works 35 normal hours plus 5 overtime hours at 1.5 times. Total pay is…":"Normal pay: 35 × 25 = 875. Overtime: 5 × 25 × 1.5 = 187.5. Total = 1,062.5.","The average of 12, 15 and 18 is…":"Average = (12 + 15 + 18) ÷ 3 = 45 ÷ 3 = 15.","A quotation differs from an estimate because a quotation is…":"A quotation is a fixed price offered before work starts. An estimate is only an approximate figure.","An accident report should be…":"An accident report should be factual, avoid blame and guesswork, and be made straight away while details are fresh.","A memo is…":"A memo is a short internal message with To, From, Date and Subject headings.","Cable 80 m at 12 per metre costs…":"Cost = 80 m × 12 = 960."};
if(!window.Z||!Z.mods)return;const seen=new Set();const fix=q=>{if(Array.isArray(q)&&E[q[0]]&&!seen.has(q)){seen.add(q);q[3]=E[q[0]]}};
[window.Y,window.Z,window.X].forEach(s=>{if(!s)return;(s.mods||[]).forEach(m=>(m.q||[]).forEach(fix));Object.keys(s).forEach(k=>{const v=s[k];if(v&&Array.isArray(v.q))v.q.forEach(fix)})});})();
